hey guys  welcome back to my youtube channel uh i  have some great news  there's a new instrument which i will be  adding to my  sort of arsenal of all things that go  pluck  and i think you're really gonna like  this one  so here we have the baroque loot  it's a  absolutely beautiful  looking instrument  this is the back here that is made up of  individual strips of wood we call ribs  in this case the wood is made out of  this gorgeous  orangey reddish flame maple  and the top is made of spruce with this  gorgeous  decoration here called a rose  the baroque loot gets its name from  existing in the baroque period of music  which is roughly 1600 to 1750.  it was popular all throughout europe  but really blossomed in france  now the instrument has 24 strings which  is a lot even for me  the fiorbo for as a point of reference  only has 14.  now although it's true that there are 24  when this is played it's really thought  of as a 13  string instrument  and that's because each string is  doubled we call them courses so there's  13 pairs of strings  and the top two are  are single  those are called the chanterelle or the  the singing strings  this specific loot was made by the  french company lelout dore or the golden  loot they're a company that makes  great quality loots for a more  affordable affordable price  and  if you're a classical guitarist and  don't want to break the bank but are  interested in learning renaissance loot  or baroque loot  this would be a great company to  start learning  now the tuning of this instrument is  part of what makes it so special  most guitars or loots are tuned in  fourths  and it requires you to use your left  hand to  fret notes to make a chord  this instrument is tuned to a chord so  if i play the open strings  we get a beautiful sounding d minor  chord and that really opens up all sorts  of new possibilities of left-hand  fingerings um because it frees up the  left hand uh in a new way allowing for  new possibilities  so the the d minor chord is really on  the first  half of the instrument  and the rest of the strings are tuned in  a descending scale which is actually  exactly what the  what the fierbo does  as well  so from a we get g  b f  d c in this case i have b flat  and a  those those notes can be  rearranged depending on the key of the  music you're playing in  this peg box is so intimidating to look  at  with 24  different friction pegs so there are no  no mechanical gears those didn't exist  until the 19th century these are  essentially like violin picks they just  they're just held in with friction  now there's some alien looking parts to  this that that's that are new to me  actually  we have this extension here  called a base rider and you can see that  the first nut stops here and the second  extra one that has two courses  gives it an extra i don't know inch or  inch and a half or maybe two inches  and that extra length actually is great  for giving the base a bit more  so i've only had a few hours since i've  got this thing in tune to start picking  away at some music since this is all  brand new to me  um but i think i've figured out the  opening of a vice star bond that i used  to play on guitar  so  i'll take a crack at it  [Music]  so  [Music]  whoa  i'm sort of blown away by  um  how this instrument feels to play uh  it's bizarre because since i know this  piece on guitar  and i'm now playing it on the instrument  for which it was written  it's so much easier on this instrument  even though i've put in my 10 000 hours  on guitar and this is my third hour or  so playing this instrument um one of the  reasons has to be because this is a d  minor suite a piece in d minor and the  instrument  is tuned to d minor  i think another thing that changes the  game is that because i have all of these  open notes  in the bass  i don't have to spend so much time in my  left hand fingering those and trying to  play a nice legato melodic line  they're just open played by my thumb  which is really similar to what i do on  fiorbo as well  besides that  i think the the lower tension strings  in fact they're very low i can really  wiggle these around um on a classical  guitar they're very very tight very high  tension  the low tension strings on this really  makes ornaments  so easy and effortless you get a sound  a sound which is uh more equal between  the pluck and the slur  and i think that really helps  helps the melody along now the notation  for  this instrument is  french tablature uh tab right so if  you're a modern guitar player tab is  when you have lines representing strings  and numbers for which fret play this is  pretty much the same thing  except it uses letters for each fret so  a b c d e f g etc and the music is  absolutely beautiful to look at  there's this incredible aesthetic  experience of playing from a facsimile  which is a picture of the manuscript the  original  handwriting of vice  it's beautiful to look at and so i'm  just loving  sight reading through this music  it's a it's a joy  [Music]  i'm absolutely in love with this  instrument  the tuning the extra bass strings  it's going to take me a lot of time to  figure this thing out but thank you for  listening today as i  learn about this instrument  tune back in because i'm going to be  posting more videos  as i get better on this instrument and  you can come learn it with me so thanks  for watching and come back for more  later

